#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Section 3 English translations (Paras 51-75) for Gabriel Marcel's
"Positions et approches concrètes du mystère ontologique"
"""

def get_sec3():
    return [
        # Para 51
        "Only this is very difficult to grasp without distortion. Inevitably I shall be tempted to react against this feeling of the interiority of the encounter, and tempted by my very honesty, tempted because from a certain point of view I must judge it as the best of myself, the surest in any case.",
        
        # Para 52
        "These clarifications risk, to tell the truth, indirectly strengthening in the minds of my listeners a preliminary objection which it is appropriate to state here as clearly as possible.",
        
        # Para 53
        "This meta-problematic, it will be said, is nevertheless a content of thought; how then can I fail to question myself about the mode of existence that belongs to it? What assures us of this existence? Is it not itself problematic in the highest degree?",
        
        # Para 54
        "My reply will be here as categorical as possible: to think or more precisely to affirm the meta-problematic is to affirm it as indubitably real, as something of which I cannot doubt without contradiction. We are here in a zone where it is no longer possible to dissociate the idea itself from the certitude or the index of certitude that affects it. For this idea is certitude, it is self-assurance, it is in that measure something other and more than an idea. But the phrase 'content of thought' which figured in the objection is as misleading as possible. For a content is after all a certain extract of experience; whereas we can elevate ourselves to the meta-problematic or to mystery only by an approach that disengages or detaches us from experience. A real disengagement; a real detachment, not at all an abstraction, that is to say a fiction recognized as such. And here we find ourselves placed before recollection, for it is in recollection and in it alone that this disengagement is accomplished. I am convinced for my part that there is no ontology possible, that is to say, no apprehension of the ontological mystery in whatever degree, except for a being capable of recollecting himself—and of bearing witness thereby that he is not a pure and simple living creature, a being abandoned to his life and without any hold upon it.",
        
        # Para 55
        "It must be observed that recollection, which has engaged pure philosophers very little, is extremely difficult to define—if only because it transcends the dualism of state and act, or more precisely because it reconciles in itself these two antinomic aspects. It is essentially the act by which I recover myself as a unity: the word itself indicates it, but this recovery, this resumption takes on the aspect of a relaxation, an abandonment. Abandonment to—relaxation in the presence of—without its being in any way possible for me to follow these prepositions by a substantive that they would govern. The road stops at the threshold...",
        
        # Para 56
        "Moreover, problematization will operate here as everywhere else, and it is the psychologist who will strive to carry it out. All that needs to be observed is that the psychologist is no more in a position to enlighten us upon the metaphysical bearing of recollection than upon the noetic value of knowing.",
        
        # Para 57
        "Within recollection I take up a position—or more precisely I place myself in a condition to take up a position—face to face with my life, I withdraw from it in some way, but not at all as the pure subject of knowledge; in this retreat I carry with me what I am and what perhaps my life is not. Here appears the gap between my being and my life. I am not my life; and if I am in a position to judge it—which I cannot deny without sinking into a radical skepticism that is nothing but despair—it is on condition of being able first to rejoin myself in recollection beyond all possible judgment, and, I would add, all representation. Recollection is doubtless what is least spectacular in the soul; it does not consist in looking at something, it is a resumption, an inward renewal, and there would be grounds for asking, I note it merely in passing, whether one should not see in it the ontological foundation of memory, the principle of effective and unrepresentable unity upon which rests the very possibility of remembering. The English phrase 'to recollect oneself' is revealing here.",
        
        # Para 58
        "It will be asked: does not recollection merge after all with that dialectical moment of turning back upon oneself or of being-for-itself (Für-sich-sein) which is at the center of German Idealism?",
        
        # Para 59
        "To tell the truth, I do not think so. To enter into oneself does not mean to be for oneself and to admire oneself, so to speak, in the intelligible unity of subject and object. On the contrary, I would say, we are here in the presence of that paradox which is mystery itself, by virtue of which the self into which I enter ceases, to that extent, to belong to itself. 'You are not your own,' this great saying of Saint Paul assumes here its meaning, at once ontological and essentially concrete; it is the one that best translates the reality around which we are circling at this moment. This reality, it will be asked, is it not an object of intuition? What you call recollection, is it not what others call intuition?",
        
        # Para 60
        "Here again it seems to me that we must be extremely prudent. If it is possible to speak here of an intuition, it is of an intuition that is not and cannot be given as such.",
        
        # Para 61
        "The more central an intuition is, the more it occupies the very depth of the being it illuminates, the less susceptible it is precisely of turning back upon itself and apprehending itself.",
        
        # Para 62
        "If we reflect further on what an intuition of being could be, we see that it is not—that it must not be—susceptible of figuring in a collection, of being catalogued as any kind of experience or Erlebnis which, on the contrary, always presents this character of being able to be now integrated, now isolated and, as it were, exposed. Consequently, every effort to remember this intuition, to picture it to oneself, I would say, can only be fruitless. In this sense, to speak to us of the intuition of being is to invite us to play upon a silent piano. This intuition cannot be brought out into the open light of day, for the simple reason that it is not, in truth, possessed.",
        
        # Para 63
        "We are here at the most difficult point of my entire exposition; instead of using the term intuition, it would be better to say that we have to do here with an assurance that underpins the whole development of thought, even discursive thought; we can therefore approach it only by a movement of conversion, that is to say, by a secondary reflection: the reflection by which I ask myself how, from what origin, the steps of an initial reflection were possible, an initial reflection that postulated the ontological, but without knowing it. This secondary reflection is recollection insofar as it is capable of thinking itself.",
        
        # Para 64
        "I certainly find it very regrettable to have to use language so abstract where it is fundamentally a question not of a dialectic ad usum philosophorum, but of what can be most vital, and, I would say, most dramatic in the rhythm of a consciousness that tests itself in order to recover itself.",
        
        # Para 65
        "It is this dramatic aspect that must now be brought to light.",
        
        # Para 66
        "Let us refer back to what I indicated almost at the beginning: the ontological exigence, the exigence of being, can deny itself. On another level, being and life do not coincide; my life, and by refraction all life, can appear to me as forever inadequate to something that I bear within me, that strictly speaking I am, but which reality nevertheless repels and excludes. Despair is possible under all forms, at every instant, in all degrees. It may seem that the very structure of our world recommends this betrayal to us, if it does not impose it. The spectacle of death which this world offers us can from a certain point of view be regarded as a perpetual incitement to denial, to absolute defection. One might even say that the permanent possibility of suicide is in this sense the perhaps essential starting point of all authentic metaphysical thought. Perhaps some will be surprised to see appearing in the midst of this abstract and completely tranquil development words so charged with affective potential, prominent words like suicide or betrayal. There is in this no concession to a taste for the sensational. I remain persuaded that it is in drama and through drama that metaphysical thought grasps itself and defines itself in concreto. M. Jacques Maritain, in a lecture given at Louvain one or two years ago on the Problem of Christian Philosophy, said: 'Nothing is easier for a philosophy than to be tragic; it has only to abandon itself to its human weight'—an allusion doubtless to the speculations of a Heidegger. I believe on the contrary that the natural incline of philosophy tends toward regions where it seems that the tragic has purely and simply disappeared, volatilized at the touch of abstract thought. And this is what we observe in many contemporary idealists. Because they ignore the person and sacrifice it to I know not what ideal truth, to I know not what anonymous principle of pure interiority, they prove incapable of embracing these tragic data of human life to which I have alluded; they expel them, along with illness and everything that falls within its domain, toward I know not what disreputable suburbs where the philosopher worthy of the name disdains to venture. But this procedure is intimately bound up, as I have said, with the refusal opposed to the ontological exigence: in reality, it is the same thing.",
        
        # Para 67
        "If I have placed the emphasis upon despair, upon betrayal, upon suicide, it is because we find in them the most manifest expressions that exist of a will to negation bearing effectively upon being.",
        
        # Para 68
        "Let us take despair, for example. We are dealing here with the act by which one despairs of reality as a whole, in the sense in which one despairs of someone. Despair presents itself as the immediate consequence or translation of a certain balance sheet: so far as I can evaluate the real—but after all, what exceeds my faculties of evaluation is for me as though it were not—I discover in it nothing that resists a process of dissolution which continues in the depth of things and which my reflection enables me to recognize and trace. At the root of despair I believe I find this affirmation: there is nothing in reality that allows me to extend credit to it; no guarantee. It is a declaration of absolute insolvency.",
        
        # Para 69
        "Hope, on the other hand, implies precisely this credit. Contrary to what Spinoza asserted, who seems to me to have confused quite distinct notions here, fear is correlative not to hope, but to desire; whereas the negative correlative of hope is the act of putting things at their worst, an act of which what has been called defeatism provides a striking illustration, and which constantly risks degenerating into a desire for the worst. Hope consists in asserting that there is in being, beyond all that is given, beyond all that can provide material for an inventory or serve as the basis for any calculation, a mysterious principle that is in connivance with me, that cannot fail to want what I want, at least if what I want truly deserves to be wanted and is in fact wanted by my whole self.",
        
        # Para 70
        "Yet it is clear that we are here at the center of what I have called the ontological mystery, and the simplest examples are also the best. To hope against all hope that the being I love will triumph over the reputedly incurable disease that undermines him, is to say: it cannot be that I am alone in willing his recovery; it is impossible that reality in its depth should be hostile or merely indifferent to what I affirm to be in itself a good. It is in vain that examples and cases calculated to discourage me are cited: beyond all experience, all probability, all statistics, I affirm that a certain order will be restored, that reality is with me in willing that it should be. I do not merely wish: I affirm; this is what I shall call the prophetic resonance of true hope.",
        
        # Para 71
        "I shall be told: 'In the immense majority of cases, there is nevertheless an illusion here.' But besides the fact that it is of the essence of the act of hope to exclude the consideration of cases, it would be necessary to show here that there exists an ascending dialectic of hope, by which hope is led to transfer itself to a plane that is transcendent in relation to all possible empirical refutations, the plane of salvation as opposed to that of success in whatever form it presents itself.",
        
        # Para 72
        "The fact remains that the correlation of hope and absolute despair persists to the end. They do not seem to me separable. I mean that the structure of the world in which we live permits, and in some way might seem to counsel, an absolute despair; but it is only in such a world that an invincible hope can arise. That is why, moreover, one cannot be too grateful to the great pessimists in the history of thought; they pushed to its limit a certain inner experience that had to be accomplished and whose radical possibility must not be masked by any apologetics; they prepared us to understand that despair could be what it was for Nietzsche (in a register, moreover, that was infra-ontological, in a zone strewn with mortal reefs): the springboard for the highest affirmation.",
        
        # Para 73
        "On the other hand, it remains certain that, in the very measure in which hope is a mystery, as Péguy saw, this mystery can be misunderstood and converted into a problem. Hope is then treated as a desire that clothes itself in illusory judgments disguising an objective reality whose true character it is in our interest to ignore. What happens here is what we noted regarding encounter or love; it is because the mystery can and in a sense must logically degrade into a problem, that an interpretation such as Spinoza's, with the confusion it implies, must itself be proposed at a given moment. So long as one remains on this side of the ontological, such an attitude cannot be open to criticism. This is very important, and it must be insisted upon. As long as I maintain toward reality the attitude of someone who is not involved in it, but considers himself bound to draw up a report as exact as possible (and this is by definition the attitude of the scientist), I am justified in observing toward this reality a kind of distrust on principle that has in principle no limit of application; such is the attitude of the laboratory worker who cannot in any degree prejudge the results of the analysis he is conducting, and who can all the better contemplate the worst because on that plane the very notion of the worst loses its meaning. But precisely an inquiry of this order, strictly comparable to that undertaken by an auditor of accounts, is pursued on this side of the order of mystery, of the order where the problem encroaches upon its own data.",
        
        # Para 74
        "In reality, when I ask myself, for instance, about the value of life, there is a profound illusion in imagining that I can still maintain this attitude: it is a genuine paralogism to think that I can pursue this inquiry as though I were not myself at stake.",
        
        # Para 75
        "Hence between hope—the reality of hope in the heart of the one whom it inhabits—and the judgments passed upon it by a mind that remains a prisoner of objectivity, there is the very same partition that separates the pure problem from the pure mystery."
    ]

